answer:
The cosine similarity \(c\text{-sim}(|y|, r)\) and the normalized magnitude estimation error (NMSE) are mathematically linked.  Defining \(q \triangleq \frac{\|r\|_2^2}{\||y|\|_2^2}\), the NMSE can be expressed as  

\[
\text{NMSE}(|y|, r) \;=\; \frac{\|r - |y|\|_2^2}{\||y|\|_2^2}
\;=\; 1 + q^2 - 2q \cdot c\text{-sim}(|y|, r) \tag{3}
\]

Thus, for comparable energy (\(q \approx 1\)), the NMSE is approximately \(2(1 - c\text{-sim})\).  This inverse relationship was observed in the steady‑state dynamic MRI data from both the OCMR and CMRxRecon2025 datasets, where high cosine similarity across cardiac phases corresponded to low NMSE at unsampled k‑space locations [1], [3].
